  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Denser Canadian wildfire smoke will finish moving \neast of the area through the afternoon.\n\nThicker smoke from Canadian wildfires will continue to exit to the \nnortheast of our area through the remainder of the afternoon into \nearly evening. However, some areas may still experience periods of \nunhealthy air quality, thus Air Quality Alerts remain in effect \nthrough this evening.\n\nKey Message 2...Showers and thunderstorms will continue into this \nevening, with numerous severe thunderstorms capable of producing \ndamaging winds and locally heavy rainfall.\n\nSevere Thunderstorm Watch in effect through 9 PM for much of Western \nand Northcentral NY.\n\nVisible imagery and composite radar showing bulk of stronger \nconvection stretching across southern Lake Ontario, the northern \nNiagara Frontier east, and southeast into the Finger Lakes region \njust ahead of the main cold front. A strong lake breeze continues \nnortheast of Lake Erie, and thus has prevented any of this \nconvection from sagging southward into the Buffalo Metro area, and \nthis may again continue to be the case through the afternoon. Most \nof the storms have been firing on a lake breeze boundary stretching \nfrom from the northern Niagara Frontier east through the counties \nliming the southern shore of Lake Ontario, and the Finger Lakes \nregion. As the cold front presses southeast across the area this \nafternoon, this entire line showers and storms (some strong to \nsevere) will move off to the southeast as well, with the main severe \nthreat ending by 7 PM this evening as the cold front sags to our \nsouth and east. With this in mind, numerous strong to severe \nthunderstorms are still expected through late afternoon/early \nevening with damaging winds, locally heavy rainfall, and an isolated \ntornado as the main threats. The SPC continues with an Enhanced Risk \n(3 of 5) of severe thunderstorms across the bulk of the area, with a \nSlight Risk now for Jefferson and Lewis counties.\n\nThe threat for thunderstorms will come to an end behind the cold \nfront later this evening, however a secondary cold front will pass \nsouth across the region later tonight with the possibility for a few \nmore showers as it passes through.",
